6A: Concepts
LangGraph models an agent as a graph that runs step by step:
State: one shared dictionary that travels through the graph (question, SQL, rows, answer). Every step reads it and writes to it.
Node: a Python function. It receives the state and returns only the keys it wants to change. LangGraph merges that into the state.
Edge: “after node A, run node B”.
Conditional edge: “after node A, call this function, and its return value picks the next node”. This is how routing and loops work.
compile(): turns your definition into a runnable app. invoke(state) runs it.
Checkpointer and thread_id: saves the state after every step, per conversation. Needed for pausing, resuming, and memory.
interrupt(): pauses the graph and waits for a human. This is how approval steps work.
Why not plain if/else functions? Graphs give you retries as loops, pause and resume, saved state, and a visible diagram. They also give Langfuse (Module 8) a natural step-by-step trace to record.
